Chapter 2 — Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Lattices and Monotone Operators

Definition 2.1 (Complete lattice). A partially ordered set (\mathcal{L}, \subseteq) is a complete lattice if every subset A \subseteq \mathcal{L} has a least upper bound \bigvee A and a greatest lower bound \bigwedge A.

Definition 2.2 (Monotone operator). An operator T : \mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{L} is monotone if A \subseteq B \Rightarrow T(A) \subseteq T(B).

Theorem 2.3 (Knaster–Tarski). Let (\mathcal{L}, \subseteq) be a complete lattice and T : \mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{L} monotone. Then T has a least fixed point \mu T and a greatest fixed point \nu T, given by

\mu T = \bigwedge \{ x : T(x) \subseteq x \}, \qquad \nu T = \bigvee \{ x : x \subseteq T(x) \}.

The Knaster–Tarski theorem guarantees existence of the greatest fixed point but does not, by itself, guarantee that the transfinite iteration T^\alpha(\top) reaches \nu T in countably many steps. Additional continuity or finiteness assumptions are required.

2.2 Distributionally Robust MDPs

Definition 2.4 (Robust MDP). A robust MDP is a tuple \mathcal{M} = (S, A, \mathfrak{P}, c, \gamma) where S is a finite state space, A a finite action space, \mathfrak{P} a family of transition kernels, c : S \times A \to \mathbb{R} a cost, and \gamma \in [0,1) a discount factor.

Definition 2.5 (Local ambiguity set). For each (s,a) \in S \times A, the local ambiguity set is

\mathcal{P}(s,a) \subseteq \Delta(S),

i.e., a set of probability distributions over the full successor state space S. Each element P(\cdot \mid s,a) \in \mathcal{P}(s,a) is a complete distribution on S. No factorization into singletons is assumed.

Definition 2.6 ((s,a)-rectangularity). A global uncertainty family \mathfrak{P} \subseteq \Delta(S)^{S \times A} is (s,a)-rectangular with respect to the local sets \{\mathcal{P}(s,a)\}_{(s,a)} if

\mathfrak{P} = \left\{ P \in \Delta(S)^{S \times A} : P(\cdot \mid s,a) \in \mathcal{P}(s,a) \ \forall (s,a) \in S \times A \right\}.

That is, the global kernel is obtained by selecting one local distribution independently for each (s,a) pair. Rectangularity is a property of the global family, not a factorization of individual distributions. Under (s,a)-rectangularity, the infimum over \mathfrak{P} separates into independent infima over each \mathcal{P}(s,a), yielding a tractable dynamic programming operator. This is the independence assumption that is a minimal requirement for most theoretical results to hold.

Definition 2.7 (Robust Bellman operator, minimization form). Under (s,a)-rectangularity and a minimization objective, define

(\mathcal{T}V)(s) = \min_{a \in A} \left[ c(s,a) + \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s' \in S} P(s' \mid s,a) V(s') \right].

The supremum represents the worst-case expected cost. This is the correct form for cost minimization.

2.3 Hybrid Viability Theory

Definition 2.8 (Viability kernel). Let X be a state space, K \subseteq X a constraint set, F : X \times U \to 2^X a set-valued dynamics, and U a control set. The viability kernel of K under F is

\operatorname{Viab}_F(K) = \{ x \in K : \exists u(\cdot) : [0,\infty) \to U, \; \forall t \geq 0, \; x(t) \in K \}.

For hybrid systems governed by impulse differential inclusions, the viability kernel is the largest closed fixed point of the predecessor operator contained in K. This characterization extends to impulse differential inclusions via the notion of viability kernel with target.

2.4 Graph Theory for Dynamic Topologies

Definition 2.9 (Dynamic graph). A dynamic graph is a sequence (G_t)_{t \geq 0} with G_t = (V_t, E_t), V_t \subseteq \mathcal{V} and E_t \subseteq \binom{V_t}{2} \cap \mathcal{E}.

Definition 2.10 (Graph-modifying action). Let \mathcal{M} be a set of modification primitives: \mathsf{add}(v), \mathsf{remove}(v), \mathsf{connect}(u,v), \mathsf{disconnect}(u,v), \mathsf{replace}(v,v'). A graph-modifying action is a word m \in \mathcal{M}^* of bounded length.

Definition 2.11 (Pending modifications). Let P_t be a multiset of in-progress modifications, each with remaining time \tau \in \mathbb{N}. The transition from G_t to G_{t+1} depends on P_t, not directly on the action m_t.

