Chapter 3 — Problem Formulation

3.1 The Physical System

We consider a modular infrastructure with a universe of module types

\mathcal{T} = \{ \text{energy}, \text{compute}, \text{storage}, \text{repair} \}

and a universe of potential modules \mathcal{V} = \{ v_1, v_2, \dots, v_{N_{\max}} \}, each with a type \tau(v) \in \mathcal{T}. At any time, only a subset V_t \subseteq \mathcal{V} is installed. Edges E_t \subseteq \binom{V_t}{2} represent communication links.

3.2 State Space

Definition 3.1 (State). The state at time t is

s_t = (G_t, P_t, z_t, h_t) \in S

where:

· G_t = (V_t, E_t) is the topology.
· P_t = \{(m_i, \tau_i)\}_{i=1}^{k_t} is the pending modification set, with k_t \leq k_{\max} and \tau_i \in \{1, \dots, \tau_{\max}\}.
· z_t = (B_t, T_t, Q_t) \in \mathbb{R}^3 is the physical state.
· h_t : V_t \to \{\mathsf{healthy}, \mathsf{degraded}, \mathsf{failed}\} is the health map.

Assumption 3.2 (Finite discretization). All components are discretized to finite sets:

B_t \in \{0, \Delta_B, \dots, B_{\max}\}, \quad
T_t \in \{0, \Delta_T, \dots, T_{\max}\}, \quad
Q_t \in \{0, \Delta_Q, \dots, Q_{\max}\}.

Thus S is finite, with |S| = N_S.

3.3 Action Space

Definition 3.3 (Action). An action at time t is

a_t = (u_t, m_t) \in A = U \times \mathcal{M}^*

where u_t \in U is an operational control and m_t \in \mathcal{M}^* is a topological modification word.

Assumption 3.4 (Bounded modification). |m_t| \leq L_{\max} for some fixed L_{\max}. This ensures A is finite.

Assumption 3.5 (Resource admissibility). An action a = (u, m) is admissible at state s = (G, P, z, h) if:

1. u \in U(s) (operational controls feasible given physical state).
2. m respects the module universe.
3. The initiation cost of m is available.
4. |P \cup \text{new}(m)| \leq k_{\max} (pending queue capacity).

Denote the admissible set A(s) \subseteq A.

3.4 Distributionally Robust Transition Dynamics

Definition 3.6 (Local ambiguity set). For each (s,a) \in S \times A, let \mathcal{P}(s,a) \subseteq \Delta(S) be a compact, convex set of probability distributions over successor states. We assume (s,a)-rectangularity in the sense of Definition 2.6.

Definition 3.7 (Transition function). For a kernel P \in \mathfrak{P}, the transition is

s_{t+1} = F(s_t, a_t, w_t), \qquad w_t \sim P(\cdot \mid s_t, a_t),

decomposed as follows.

Topology update.

(G_{t+1}, P_{t+1}) = \Phi(G_t, P_t, m_t)

where \Phi advances pending modifications by one step, applies completed modifications, and enqueues new modifications.

Physical update. z_{t+1} = f_{\mathrm{phys}}(z_t, G_{t+1}, u_t, w_t) with

B_{t+1} = B_t + \eta_{\mathrm{prod}}(G_{t+1}, w_t) \Delta t - \eta_{\mathrm{cons}}(u_t, G_{t+1}) \Delta t

T_{t+1} = T_t + \frac{Q_{\mathrm{in}}(u_t, G_{t+1}) - Q_{\mathrm{out}}(G_{t+1}, T_t)}{C_{\mathrm{th}}} \Delta t

Q_{t+1} = Q_t + d_t - \eta_{\mathrm{proc}}(u_t, G_{t+1}) \Delta t

Health update.

h_{t+1}(v) = \begin{cases} \mathsf{failed} & \text{if } v \text{ fails under } w_t \\ \mathsf{degraded} & \text{if } h_t(v) = \mathsf{healthy} \text{ and degradation occurs} \\ h_t(v) & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

3.5 Admissible Set

Definition 3.8 (Topology-dependent admissible set). For a topology G,

K(G) = \left\{ (G, P, z, h) \in S : \begin{array}{l} B \geq B_{\min}(G) \\ T \leq T_{\max}(G) \\ \text{connectivity}(G) \text{ holds} \\ \text{health}(h) \text{ permits operation} \end{array} \right\}

The global admissible set is

\tilde{K} = \{ s = (G, \cdot) : s \in K(G) \} \subseteq \bigcup_G K(G).

We use \tilde{K} as the constraint set throughout.

3.6 Cost and Service

Definition 3.9 (Vector-valued outcome). For each transition (s,a),

c(s,a) = \big( c_{\mathrm{cost}}(s,a), \; c_{\mathrm{service}}(s,a), \; c_{\mathrm{viol}}(s,a) \big) \in \mathbb{R}^3

with c_{\mathrm{cost}} the instantaneous cost, c_{\mathrm{service}} computation delivered (negative, since we minimize), and c_{\mathrm{viol}} the indicator of constraint violation.

Definition 3.10 (Discounted vector objective). For a policy \pi and initial state s,

J^\pi(s) = \mathbb{E}^\pi \left[ \sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t c(s_t, a_t) \,\middle|\, s_0 = s \right] \in \mathbb{R}^3.

3.7 Policies

Definition 3.11 (Policy classes).

· \Pi_{\mathrm{base}}: stationary policies with m_t = \emptyset, no viability constraint.
· \Pi_{\mathrm{free}}: history-dependent policies with full action space, no viability constraint.
· \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}}: policies with m_t = \emptyset satisfying the robust viability constraint.
· \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}: policies with full action space satisfying the robust viability constraint.
· \Pi_{\mathrm{myope}}: policies with horizon H_m, no viability constraint.

