Chapter 4 — Distributionally Robust Viability Kernels

4.1 The Core Difficulty

Standard viability theory assumes a fixed constraint set K. Here, K depends on the topology G, which is itself a decision variable. A topological modification can expand K, contract K, or leave it unchanged.

4.2 Distributionally Robust Viability Operator

Definition 4.1 (Robust viability operator). Define T_{\mathcal{P}} : 2^S \to 2^S by

T_{\mathcal{P}}(V) = \left\{ s \in \tilde{K} : \exists a \in A(s), \; \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V \mid s,a) = 1 \right\}.

Lemma 4.2 (Monotonicity). T_{\mathcal{P}} is monotone: V_1 \subseteq V_2 \Rightarrow T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_1) \subseteq T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_2).

Proof. Let s \in T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_1). Then \exists a, \forall P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a), P(V_1 \mid s,a) = 1. Since V_1 \subseteq V_2, P(V_2 \mid s,a) \geq P(V_1 \mid s,a) = 1, so P(V_2 \mid s,a) = 1. Hence s \in T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_2). \square

Lemma 4.3 (Contraction toward \tilde{K}). T_{\mathcal{P}}(V) \subseteq \tilde{K} for all V \subseteq S.

4.3 Infinite-Horizon Viability Kernel — Direct Finiteness Proof

Definition 4.4 (Infinite-horizon kernel). Define the decreasing sequence

V_0 = \tilde{K}, \qquad V_{n+1} = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_n).

The infinite-horizon viability kernel is

V_\infty = \bigcap_{n \geq 0} V_n.

Theorem 4.5 (Convergence in finite time). Let S be finite, let T_{\mathcal{P}} be monotone with T_{\mathcal{P}}(V) \subseteq \tilde{K} for all V, and define V_0 = \tilde{K}, V_{n+1} = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_n). Then:

1. The sequence (V_n) is decreasing: V_{n+1} \subseteq V_n for all n.
2. The sequence stabilizes in at most |S| strictly decreasing steps: there exists n^* \leq |S| such that V_{n^*+1} = V_{n^*}.
3. The limit V_\infty = V_{n^*} is the greatest fixed point of T_{\mathcal{P}} contained in \tilde{K}.

Proof.

(1) V_1 = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_0) = T_{\mathcal{P}}(\tilde{K}) \subseteq \tilde{K} = V_0. By monotonicity and induction, V_{n+1} \subseteq V_n.

(2) If V_{n+1} \subsetneq V_n, then at least one state is removed. Since |S| is finite, at most |S| strict decreases can occur before stabilization.

(3) Let V_{n^*} be the stabilized set. Then T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_{n^*}) = V_{n^*}, so V_{n^*} is a fixed point contained in \tilde{K}. Now let W \subseteq \tilde{K} be any fixed point: W \subseteq T_{\mathcal{P}}(W). We show W \subseteq V_n for all n by induction. Base: W \subseteq \tilde{K} = V_0. Step: if W \subseteq V_n, then W = T_{\mathcal{P}}(W) \subseteq T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_n) = V_{n+1} by monotonicity. Hence W \subseteq V_{n^*} = V_\infty. Therefore V_\infty is the greatest fixed point contained in \tilde{K}. \square

This proof replaces reliance on Kleene iteration, which required continuity not guaranteed by Knaster–Tarski alone. The finiteness of S provides a simpler and stronger argument.

Corollary 4.6 (Empty kernel). V_\infty = \emptyset if and only if for every s \in \tilde{K} and every policy, there exists a kernel P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a) under which the system eventually leaves \tilde{K} with positive probability.

4.4 One-Step Probabilistic Constraint

Definition 4.7 (One-step probabilistic viability operator). For \beta \in [0,1], define

T_{\mathcal{P}}^\beta(V) = \left\{ s \in \tilde{K} : \exists a \in A(s), \; \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V \mid s,a) \geq 1 - \beta \right\}.

Proposition 4.8 (Monotonicity in \beta). If \beta_1 \leq \beta_2, then the greatest fixed point of T_{\mathcal{P}}^{\beta_1} contains that of T_{\mathcal{P}}^{\beta_2}.

Proof. Immediate from 1 - \beta_1 \geq 1 - \beta_2. \square

Remark 4.9 (Interpretation). The operator T_{\mathcal{P}}^\beta enforces a one-step probabilistic constraint: at each step, the probability of transitioning to the viable set is at least 1 - \beta. This does not imply that the cumulative probability of violation over an infinite horizon is bounded by \beta. A genuine infinite-horizon risk constraint requires a separate construction, e.g., a constrained MDP with a cumulative cost c_{\mathrm{viol}} and a budget \beta / (1-\gamma).

4.5 Existence of Viable and Optimal Viable Policies

Theorem 4.10 (Existence of viable policy). If s_0 \in V_\infty, then there exists a stationary deterministic policy \pi^* that is viable from s_0.

Proof. By V_\infty = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_\infty), for every s \in V_\infty there exists a \in A(s) with \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V_\infty \mid s,a) = 1. Selecting such a defines \pi^*. Inductively, s_t \in V_\infty \subseteq \tilde{K}. \square

Theorem 4.11 (Existence of optimal viable policy). Let A(s) be finite for all s \in V_\infty, let c be bounded, and let \gamma < 1. Define the viability-restricted action set

A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) = \left\{ a \in A(s) : \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V_\infty \mid s,a) = 1 \right\}.

If A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) \neq \emptyset for all s \in V_\infty, then there exists a stationary deterministic policy \pi^* \in \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} attaining

V^*(s) = \min_{\pi \in \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}} J^\pi(s)

for all s \in V_\infty, and V^* satisfies the robust Bellman equation

V^*(s) = \min_{a \in A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s)} \left[ c(s,a) + \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s' \in S} P(s' \mid s,a) V^*(s') \right].

Proof. The restricted action sets A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) are finite and non-empty. The operator on the right-hand side is a contraction on the Banach space of bounded functions on V_\infty with modulus \gamma. The Banach fixed-point theorem yields a unique fixed point V^*, and the standard MDP argument yields a stationary deterministic optimal policy. \square

Remark 4.12. This theorem establishes existence for the scalarized minimization problem. It does not, by itself, establish properties of the multi-objective Pareto frontier; those require separate treatment.

4.6 Viability Across Topological Transitions

Definition 4.13 (Topological viability). A state s \in K(G) is topologically viable if there exists a policy \pi such that for all kernel selections P_t \in \mathcal{P}(s_t, a_t), the trajectory satisfies s_t \in K(G_t) for all t \geq 0.

Proposition 4.14. V_\infty as defined in 4.4 is exactly the set of topologically viable states.

Proof. Direct from Definition 3.8 and the fact that F produces s' with G' explicitly. \square

