Chapter 5 — Existence and Structure Theorems

5.1 Structure of the Viability Kernel

Theorem 5.1 (Closedness). If A(s) is non-empty for all s \in V_\infty and \mathcal{P}(s,a) is compact for all (s,a), then V_\infty is closed.

Proof. \tilde{K} is closed (finite set). T_{\mathcal{P}} preserves closedness: for a closed V, T_{\mathcal{P}}(V) is defined by finite conjunctions/disjunctions of closed conditions. By induction, V_n closed for all n. Intersection of closed sets is closed. \square

In a finite state space, every subset is both open and closed, so the theorem is trivially true. The statement is included for structural completeness and for potential extension to infinite state spaces.

Theorem 5.2 (Absorbing property). V_\infty is absorbing under \pi^*: for s \in V_\infty, \pi^*(s) \in A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) and \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s, \pi^*(s))} P(V_\infty \mid s, \pi^*(s)) = 1.

Proof. Immediate from \pi^* definition and V_\infty = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_\infty). \square

5.2 The Role of Topology — Conditional Monotonicity

Definition 5.3 (Reachability of topologies). For topologies G, G' and state s \in K(G), write G \rightsquigarrow G' if there exists a modification word m and a sequence of kernels such that a trajectory from s reaches some s' \in K(G') with topology G'.

Remark 5.4. The claim that adding modules cannot decrease B_{\min}, cannot increase T_{\max}, and can only improve connectivity is false as a general physical law. An additional module can increase energy demand, introduce thermal load, create new failure modes, and complicate communication routing. Therefore G \rightsquigarrow G' does not imply K(G) \subseteq K(G').

Proposition 5.5 (Conditional topological monotonicity). Let G' be obtained from G by adding a module v and connecting it. Suppose the following non-degradation conditions hold:

1. Energy: B_{\min}(G') \leq B_{\min}(G) and for all z in the embedded state space, the net energy production \eta_{\mathrm{prod}} does not decrease.
2. Thermal: T_{\max}(G') \geq T_{\max}(G) and the heat dissipation coefficient C_{\mathrm{th}} does not decrease.
3. Connectivity: the connectivity predicate for G' implies that for G under the natural embedding.
4. Health: the health predicate for G' restricted to V(G) is at least as permissive as that for G.

Then, under the natural embedding \iota : S_G \hookrightarrow S_{G'} that maps (G, P, z, h) to (G', P, z, h \cup \{v \mapsto \mathsf{healthy}\}), we have

\iota(K(G)) \subseteq K(G').

Proof. Each condition of Definition 3.8 is preserved by the corresponding non-degradation assumption. The embedding adds a healthy module and preserves all other components. \square

Remark 5.6. This proposition is conditional. It does not assert that adding modules always expands the admissible set; it asserts that under explicit physical conditions, the expansion holds. The non-degradation conditions must be verified for each candidate module.

Theorem 5.7 (Topological viability ≠ physical viability). There exist systems where V_\infty \neq \emptyset but the projection of V_\infty onto physical variables is strictly smaller than the viability kernel of any fixed topology.

Proof sketch. Consider a system where the only way to remain viable is to alternate between two topologies: G_1 has high energy but low compute, G_2 has low energy but high compute. Neither fixed topology is viable, but the coupled policy is. This construction is realizable in the three-module minimal model. \square

Corollary 5.8 (Necessity of topology change). There exist systems where \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} = \emptyset but \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} \neq \emptyset.

5.3 Complexity Analysis

Theorem 5.9 (Operation count for kernel computation). Let S be finite, let |A| = \max_s |A(s)|, and let \mathcal{P}(s,a) have a finite extreme-point representation with at most |\mathcal{P}| extreme points. Then computing V_\infty by the kernel iteration algorithm requires at most

O\left( |S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}| \right)

elementary operations, where each elementary operation is an evaluation of P(V \mid s,a) for an extreme kernel P.

Proof. The sequence V_n stabilizes in at most |S| steps (Theorem 4.5). Each step requires, for each s \in S and a \in A(s), evaluating \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V_n \mid s,a). Under (s,a)-rectangularity, this infimum is attained at an extreme point of \mathcal{P}(s,a), of which there are at most |\mathcal{P}|. \square

Remark 5.10 (Machine-encoded complexity). The bound above counts elementary operations. It does not account for the cost of solving the inner optimization \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V \mid s,a) when \mathcal{P}(s,a) is described by, e.g., linear inequalities. For polytopal ambiguity sets, each inner problem is a linear program whose cost must be added. A fully machine-encoded complexity bound remains open.

Open Problem 5.11 (PSPACE-hardness). To prove PSPACE-hardness of the general GM-MDP viability problem, one must exhibit a polynomial-time reduction from a known PSPACE-hard problem to the decision problem

\text{GM-VIAB} = \{ (S, A, \mathfrak{P}, \tilde{K}) : V_\infty \neq \emptyset \}.

Such a reduction must preserve the graph-modifying structure and the topology-dependent constraint sets. This remains open. For (s,a)-rectangular RMDPs with polytopic uncertainty, the threshold problem is in NP and robust policy evaluation is in P. For s-rectangular RMDPs, the threshold problem is in PSPACE via the first-order theory of the reals. A polynomial-time algorithm for the RMDP threshold problem would resolve the long-standing open question of whether parity games can be solved in polynomial time.

