Chapter 6 — Value Decomposition and the Price of Safety

6.1 The Four Policies and Their Values

Define the four policies of Definition 3.11 and their optimal minimization values over a horizon H with discount \gamma:

· J_{\mathrm{base}}(s): optimal value with m \equiv \emptyset, no viability constraint.
· J_{\mathrm{free}}(s): optimal value with full actions, no viability constraint.
· J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}}(s): optimal value with m \equiv \emptyset, subject to viability.
· J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}(s): optimal value with full actions, subject to viability.

All values are defined on the same initial state, horizon, uncertainty family, and cost function.

6.2 Algebraic Decomposition

Proposition 6.1 (Policy inclusion). The following inclusions hold:

\Pi_{\mathrm{base}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{free}}, \qquad
\Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}.

Consequently, under minimization,

J_{\mathrm{free}} \leq J_{\mathrm{base}}, \qquad
J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} \leq J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}}.

Proof. The first inclusion holds because m \equiv \emptyset is a special case of the full action space. The second holds because fixed-topology viable policies are a subset of coupled viable policies. The inequalities follow from minimization over a larger set. \square

Definition 6.2 (Algebraic decomposition).

\underbrace{J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} - J_{\mathrm{base}}}_{\text{total improvement}}
=
\underbrace{(J_{\mathrm{free}} - J_{\mathrm{base}})}_{\text{value of topological freedom}}
+
\underbrace{(J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} - J_{\mathrm{free}})}_{\text{price of safety (coupled)}}
+
\underbrace{(J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} - J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}})}_{\text{value of coupling under safety}}.

Remark 6.3. This is an algebraic identity, not a causal separation. The middle term mixes the effect of enforcing safety with the effect of changing topology. The third term measures a cost difference between two viable policy classes; it is not a geometric property of the viability kernel alone.

6.3 The Value of Coupling

Definition 6.4 (Value of coupling).

\Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} - J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}.

Proposition 6.5 (Non-negativity). \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} \geq 0.

Proof. Immediate from \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} under minimization. \square

Remark 6.6 (Counterexample to equality condition). The claim that \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = 0 if and only if the viability constraints under fixed and coupled topology are equivalent is false. Consider a single initial state with two viable policies:

· Fixed policy: cost J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} = 5.
· Coupled policy: cost J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} = 5, with a topological change available that does not improve cost.

Then \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = 0, although the policy classes and the available topological modifications are different. Conversely, a strict gap \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} > 0 may arise from operational cost differences without a strict inclusion of viability kernels.

Proposition 6.7 (Corrected interpretation). If the objective is a minimization and \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}, then

\Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} - J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} \geq 0.

Equality \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = 0 means that the two policy classes attain the same optimal value for the considered objective. It does not imply that the policy classes, the reachable sets, or the viability kernels are identical.

6.4 Accessibility and Strict Coupling

Definition 6.8 (Accessibility). A state s' \in V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} \setminus V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}} is accessible from s_0 if there exists a coupled viable policy and a kernel selection under which s' is reached with positive probability from s_0.

Theorem 6.9 (Strict coupling value). \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}}(s_0) > 0 if and only if there exists an accessible state s' \in V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} \setminus V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}} from which the coupled policy achieves strictly lower cost than any fixed-topology viable policy can achieve from s_0.

Proof. If no such accessible state exists, then any coupled viable trajectory from s_0 can be mimicked by a fixed-topology viable policy up to cost, so \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}}(s_0) = 0. Conversely, if such a state exists, the coupled policy can exploit it to reduce cost strictly. \square

Corollary 6.10. Coupling matters strictly if and only if the topology is a non-trivial control variable and the expanded viability kernel is accessible from the initial state and the expansion yields a strict cost improvement.

6.5 Pareto Formulation

Definition 6.11 (Pareto-optimal policy set).

\Pi^* = \{ \pi \in \Pi : \nexists \pi', J(\pi') \preceq J(\pi), J(\pi') \neq J(\pi) \}.

Proposition 6.12 (Structure of Pareto frontier). For a finite state space, a finite action space, a finite set of policies, and a finite representation of the ambiguity set, the Pareto frontier \{J(\pi) : \pi \in \Pi^*\} is a finite subset of \mathbb{R}^3.

Proof. The set of deterministic stationary policies is finite. The Pareto frontier of a finite set is finite. \square

Remark 6.13. The multi-objective dynamic programming algorithm must specify how it treats distributional uncertainty, adversarial kernel selection, and trade-offs between objectives. A straightforward extension replaces the scalar Bellman operator with a set-valued operator whose output is pruned by Pareto dominance. The worst-case size of the Pareto set can be exponential in the horizon; limited-precision approaches yield tractable approximations.

6.6 Value of Information

Definition 6.14 (Value of information). Let J^{\pi^*}_{\text{full}} be the optimal value under full information and J^{\pi^*}_{\text{partial}} the optimal value under partial information. Define

\mathrm{VoI} = J^{\pi^*}_{\text{partial}} - J^{\pi^*}_{\text{full}}.

Proposition 6.15. \mathrm{VoI} \geq 0. Equality holds if and only if the optimal open-loop policy is optimal closed-loop.

Proof. Under full information, the policy can reproduce any partially informed decision. Under minimization, more information cannot increase the optimal cost. \square

