Chapter 8 — Approximate Algorithms

8.1 State Aggregation

Definition 8.1 (Aggregation map). \phi : S \to \bar{S} is a surjection. The aggregated kernel is

\bar{T}(\bar{V}) = \{ \bar{s} \in \bar{K} : \exists a, \forall P, \phi(F(s,a,w)) \in \bar{V} \text{ for all } s \in \phi^{-1}(\bar{s}) \}.

Theorem 8.2 (Soundness). \phi(V_\infty) \subseteq \bar{V}_\infty. In general, equality fails.

8.2 Monte Carlo Rollouts

Sampling bound. Let \delta \in (0,1) be the target one-sided confidence level and \alpha \in (0,1) the significance level. To estimate a probability p with additive error \delta and confidence 1-\alpha, Hoeffding's inequality requires

N \geq \frac{1}{2\delta^2} \log(2/\alpha)

samples. Here \delta is the statistical precision and \alpha is the confidence parameter; they are distinct.

Robust viability caveat. Sampling under a nominal distribution P_0 \in \mathcal{P}(s,a) does not certify robust viability under all P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a). To certify robust viability by simulation, one must sample from the worst-case kernel or use a robust optimization procedure over \mathcal{P}(s,a).

8.3 Reinforcement Learning with Shielding

When |S| is too large, use deep RL with a shield: a runtime monitor that rejects actions that would leave V_\infty (approximated). This is the safe RL paradigm. Constrained multi-objective RL frameworks provide primal-based methods that orchestrate policy optimization between multiple objectives and constraint adherence.

