Chapter 10 — Falsification Protocol

10.1 Three Levels of Conclusion

Level 1 — Local refutation. A counterexample to a universal proposition invalidates that proposition under its stated hypotheses. Example: if a specific physical system violates Proposition 5.5's non-degradation conditions and K(G) \not\subseteq K(G'), then Proposition 5.5 is correctly conditional; the counterexample does not refute the conditional statement but clarifies that the conditions are not automatic.

Level 2 — Bounded experimental result. A hypothesis not verified on a specific parameter grid is not verified on that grid. It does not follow that the hypothesis is false universally. Example: if H1 fails on all configurations of the three-module model, H1 is not verified in that model; the general theory is not refuted.

Level 3 — Theoretical result. A proof establishes a property for the entire class of systems defined by the hypotheses. Example: Theorem 4.5 (convergence in finite time) is a Level 3 result for finite state spaces.

10.2 Refutation Targets

Hypothesis Refutation criterion Level
H1 No initial state exhibits the trap under any parameter setting Level 2
H2 V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} = V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}} for all parameters Level 2
H3 \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = 0 for all initial states and parameters Level 2
H4 Pareto frontier collapses to a single point Level 2

10.3 Parameter Sensitivity

Parameters to vary:

