Appendix A — Lattice Theory and Fixed Points

A.1 Proof of the Knaster–Tarski Theorem

Theorem A.1 (Knaster–Tarski). Let (\mathcal{L}, \subseteq) be a complete lattice and T : \mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{L} monotone. Then T has a least fixed point \mu T and a greatest fixed point \nu T, given by

\mu T = \bigwedge \{ x \in \mathcal{L} : T(x) \subseteq x \}, \qquad
\nu T = \bigvee \{ x \in \mathcal{L} : x \subseteq T(x) \}.

Proof. We prove the result for the greatest fixed point; the least fixed point follows by duality. Let

P = \{ x \in \mathcal{L} : x \subseteq T(x) \}

be the set of post-fixed points. Since \mathcal{L} is a complete lattice, P has a supremum p^* = \bigvee P. We show that p^* \in P and that p^* is the greatest fixed point.

First, for any x \in P, we have x \subseteq p^*. By monotonicity of T, T(x) \subseteq T(p^*). Since x \subseteq T(x), it follows that x \subseteq T(p^*). Hence p^* = \bigvee P \subseteq T(p^*), so p^* \in P.

Now, since p^* \in P, we have p^* \subseteq T(p^*). Applying T to both sides and using monotonicity, T(p^*) \subseteq T(T(p^*)). Thus T(p^*) \in P, so T(p^*) \subseteq \bigvee P = p^*. Combined with p^* \subseteq T(p^*), we obtain T(p^*) = p^*. Therefore p^* is a fixed point.

Finally, let q be any fixed point of T. Then q \subseteq T(q), so q \in P, hence q \subseteq p^*. Thus p^* is the greatest fixed point. \square

A.2 Monotone Operators on Finite Lattices

Lemma A.2. Let S be a finite set and let T : 2^S \to 2^S be monotone. Define the sequence V_0 = S, V_{n+1} = T(V_n). Then:

1. V_{n+1} \subseteq V_n for all n \geq 0.
2. There exists n^* \leq |S| such that V_{n^*+1} = V_{n^*}.
3. V_{n^*} is the greatest fixed point of T.

Proof. (1) V_1 = T(V_0) = T(S) \subseteq S = V_0. If V_n \subseteq V_{n-1}, then by monotonicity V_{n+1} = T(V_n) \subseteq T(V_{n-1}) = V_n. By induction, the sequence is decreasing.

(2) If V_{n+1} \subsetneq V_n, then |V_{n+1}| < |V_n|. Since |S| is finite, at most |S| strict decreases can occur before stabilization.

(3) Let V_{n^*} = V_{n^*+1} = T(V_{n^*}). Then V_{n^*} is a fixed point. Let W be any fixed point of T. We show W \subseteq V_n for all n by induction. Base: W \subseteq S = V_0. Step: if W \subseteq V_n, then W = T(W) \subseteq T(V_n) = V_{n+1} by monotonicity. Hence W \subseteq V_{n^*}, so V_{n^*} is the greatest fixed point. \square

