Appendix B — Distributionally Robust MDPs

B.1 Rectangularity and Separation of Infima

Definition B.1. A global uncertainty family \mathfrak{P} \subseteq \Delta(S)^{S \times A} is (s,a)-rectangular with respect to local sets \{\mathcal{P}(s,a)\}_{(s,a)} if

\mathfrak{P} = \left\{ P \in \Delta(S)^{S \times A} : P(\cdot \mid s,a) \in \mathcal{P}(s,a) \ \forall (s,a) \in S \times A \right\}.

Lemma B.2 (Separation). Under (s,a)-rectangularity, for any function V : S \to \mathbb{R},

\inf_{P \in \mathfrak{P}} \sum_{s' \in S} P(s' \mid s,a) V(s') = \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s' \in S} P(s' \mid s,a) V(s').

Proof. Since the choice of local distribution at each (s,a) is independent, the infimum over the product family separates into independent infima. For a fixed (s,a), the value of P(\cdot \mid s,a) does not affect any other state-action pair, so the global infimum is attained by minimizing each local term separately. \square

B.2 Robust Bellman Operator and Contraction

Definition B.3. Under (s,a)-rectangularity, the robust Bellman operator for cost minimization is

(\mathcal{T}V)(s) = \min_{a \in A} \left[ c(s,a) + \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s' \in S} P(s' \mid s,a) V(s') \right].

Lemma B.4 (Contraction). The operator \mathcal{T} is a contraction in the supremum norm with modulus \gamma.

Proof. Let V, W : S \to \mathbb{R}. For any s \in S,

|(\mathcal{T}V)(s) - (\mathcal{T}W)(s)| \leq \max_{a \in A} \left| \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s'} P(s' \mid s,a) V(s') - \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s'} P(s' \mid s,a) W(s') \right|.

For any P, |\sum_{s'} P(s' \mid s,a) (V(s') - W(s'))| \leq \|V - W\|_\infty. The supremum over P is non-expansive, so

\left| \sup_P \sum_{s'} P V - \sup_P \sum_{s'} P W \right| \leq \|V - W\|_\infty.

Thus |(\mathcal{T}V)(s) - (\mathcal{T}W)(s)| \leq \gamma \|V - W\|_\infty. Taking the supremum over s yields \|\mathcal{T}V - \mathcal{T}W\|_\infty \leq \gamma \|V - W\|_\infty. \square

Corollary B.5. For \gamma < 1, \mathcal{T} has a unique fixed point V^*, and value iteration converges to V^* geometrically.

