Appendix C — Viability Kernel Proofs

C.1 Proof of Theorem 4.5 (Convergence in Finite Time)

Theorem C.1. Let S be finite, let T_{\mathcal{P}} : 2^S \to 2^S be monotone with T_{\mathcal{P}}(V) \subseteq \tilde{K} for all V \subseteq S, and define V_0 = \tilde{K}, V_{n+1} = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_n). Then:

1. V_{n+1} \subseteq V_n for all n.
2. There exists n^* \leq |S| such that V_{n^*+1} = V_{n^*}.
3. V_\infty = V_{n^*} is the greatest fixed point of T_{\mathcal{P}} contained in \tilde{K}.

Proof. (1) V_1 = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_0) = T_{\mathcal{P}}(\tilde{K}) \subseteq \tilde{K} = V_0. If V_n \subseteq V_{n-1}, then by monotonicity V_{n+1} = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_n) \subseteq T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_{n-1}) = V_n. By induction, the sequence is decreasing.

(2) If V_{n+1} \subsetneq V_n, then at least one state is removed, so |V_{n+1}| < |V_n|. Since |S| is finite, at most |S| strict decreases can occur before stabilization.

(3) Let V_{n^*} = V_{n^*+1} = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_{n^*}). Then V_{n^*} is a fixed point contained in \tilde{K}. Let W \subseteq \tilde{K} be any fixed point of T_{\mathcal{P}}. We show W \subseteq V_n for all n by induction. Base: W \subseteq \tilde{K} = V_0. Step: if W \subseteq V_n, then W = T_{\mathcal{P}}(W) \subseteq T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_n) = V_{n+1} by monotonicity. Hence W \subseteq V_{n^*}. Therefore V_{n^*} is the greatest fixed point contained in \tilde{K}. \square

C.2 Proof of Theorem 4.10 (Existence of Viable Policy)

Theorem C.2. If s_0 \in V_\infty, then there exists a stationary deterministic policy \pi^* that is viable from s_0.

Proof. Since V_\infty = T_{\mathcal{P}}(V_\infty), for every s \in V_\infty there exists a \in A(s) such that

\inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V_\infty \mid s,a) = 1.

Choose such an action a^*(s) for each s \in V_\infty. Define \pi^*(s) = a^*(s). Then for any kernel selection P \in \mathcal{P}(s, \pi^*(s)), the next state lies in V_\infty with probability 1. By induction, s_t \in V_\infty \subseteq \tilde{K} for all t \geq 0. \square

C.3 Proof of Theorem 4.11 (Existence of Optimal Viable Policy)

Theorem C.3. Let A(s) be finite for all s \in V_\infty, let c be bounded, and let \gamma < 1. Define

A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) = \left\{ a \in A(s) : \inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V_\infty \mid s,a) = 1 \right\}.

If A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) \neq \emptyset for all s \in V_\infty, then there exists a stationary deterministic policy \pi^* \in \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} attaining

V^*(s) = \min_{\pi \in \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}} J^\pi(s)

for all s \in V_\infty, and V^* satisfies the robust Bellman equation

V^*(s) = \min_{a \in A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s)} \left[ c(s,a) + \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s' \in S} P(s' \mid s,a) V^*(s') \right].

Proof. The restricted action sets A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) are finite and non-empty. Define the operator \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{safe}} on the Banach space of bounded functions on V_\infty by

(\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{safe}} V)(s) = \min_{a \in A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s)} \left[ c(s,a) + \gamma \sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s' \in S} P(s' \mid s,a) V(s') \right].

By the same argument as Lemma B.4, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{safe}} is a contraction with modulus \gamma. By the Banach fixed-point theorem, it has a unique fixed point V^*. The standard MDP argument (Puterman, 1994, Theorem 6.2.10) yields a stationary deterministic optimal policy \pi^* that attains the minimum in the Bellman equation. \square

