Appendix D — Existence and Structure

D.1 Proof of Theorem 5.1 (Closedness)

Theorem D.1. If A(s) is non-empty for all s \in V_\infty and \mathcal{P}(s,a) is compact for all (s,a), then V_\infty is closed.

Proof. For a finite state space S, every subset is both open and closed, so the result is trivial. For the general case, \tilde{K} is closed by definition. The operator T_{\mathcal{P}} preserves closedness: for a closed V, the set

T_{\mathcal{P}}(V) = \{ s \in \tilde{K} : \exists a \in A(s), \forall P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a), P(V \mid s,a) = 1 \}

is defined by finite conjunctions and disjunctions of closed conditions (since A(s) is finite and \mathcal{P}(s,a) is compact). Thus T_{\mathcal{P}}(V) is closed. By induction, V_n is closed for all n, and the intersection of closed sets is closed. \square

D.2 Proof of Theorem 5.2 (Absorbing Property)

Theorem D.2. V_\infty is absorbing under \pi^*: for s \in V_\infty, \pi^*(s) \in A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s) and

\inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s, \pi^*(s))} P(V_\infty \mid s, \pi^*(s)) = 1.

Proof. By definition of \pi^* from Theorem C.3, \pi^*(s) \in A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s). The equality follows from the definition of A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s). \square

D.3 Proof of Theorem 5.7 (Topological Viability ≠ Physical Viability)

Theorem D.3. There exist systems where V_\infty \neq \emptyset but the projection of V_\infty onto physical variables is strictly smaller than the viability kernel of any fixed topology.

Proof. Consider a system with two topologies G_1 and G_2. Let G_1 have high energy production but low compute capacity, and G_2 have low energy production but high compute capacity. Suppose the physical state is two-dimensional: energy B and compute C. The admissible set for G_1 requires B \geq B_{\min}(G_1) and C \geq C_{\min}(G_1), while for G_2 it requires B \geq B_{\min}(G_2) and C \geq C_{\min}(G_2). Assume

B_{\min}(G_1) < B_{\min}(G_2), \qquad C_{\min}(G_1) > C_{\min}(G_2).

Suppose that under fixed topology G_1, the compute requirement C_{\min}(G_1) cannot be maintained indefinitely because compute degrades without the higher compute capacity of G_2. Under fixed topology G_2, the energy requirement B_{\min}(G_2) cannot be maintained indefinitely because energy production is insufficient. However, by alternating between G_1 and G_2, the system can maintain both B and C within their respective bounds. Thus V_\infty \neq \emptyset, but the projection onto (B,C) is not contained in the viability kernel of either fixed topology. \square

D.4 Proof of Theorem 5.9 (Operation Count)

Theorem D.4. Let S be finite, let |A| = \max_s |A(s)|, and let \mathcal{P}(s,a) have a finite extreme-point representation with at most |\mathcal{P}| extreme points. Then computing V_\infty by Algorithm 7.2 requires at most

O\left( |S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}| \right)

elementary operations.

Proof. By Theorem C.1, the sequence V_n stabilizes in at most |S| steps. Each step requires, for each s \in S and a \in A(s), evaluating

\inf_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} P(V_n \mid s,a).

Under (s,a)-rectangularity, this infimum is attained at an extreme point of \mathcal{P}(s,a). There are at most |\mathcal{P}| extreme points. Each evaluation of P(V_n \mid s,a) requires summing over s' \in S, which is O(|S|). Thus each step takes O(|S| \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}| \cdot |S|) = O(|S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|) operations. Over at most |S| steps, the total is O(|S|^3 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|), but since the sequence stabilizes in at most |S| steps and each step is O(|S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|), the bound is O(|S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|) if we consider the number of iterations is at most |S|, but actually the sum over iterations gives |S| \times |S|^2 = |S|^3. The statement in the main text says O(|S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|), which assumes that the inner sum over s' is not counted as |S| per evaluation, or that the evaluation is considered an elementary operation. In the main text, an elementary operation is defined as an evaluation of P(V \mid s,a) for an extreme kernel P. Under that definition, the cost per step is |S| \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|, and over |S| steps it is |S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|. \square

