Appendix E — Value Decomposition

E.1 Proof of Proposition 6.1 (Policy Inclusion)

Proposition E.1. \Pi_{\mathrm{base}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{free}} and \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}. Consequently, under minimization,

J_{\mathrm{free}} \leq J_{\mathrm{base}}, \qquad J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} \leq J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}}.

Proof. The first inclusion holds because m \equiv \emptyset is a special case of the full action space. The second holds because fixed-topology viable policies are a subset of coupled viable policies. The inequalities follow from minimization over a larger set. \square

E.2 Proof of Proposition 6.5 (Non-Negativity of Coupling Value)

Proposition E.2. \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} - J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} \geq 0.

Proof. Immediate from \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} under minimization. \square

E.3 Proof of Proposition 6.7 (Corrected Interpretation)

Proposition E.3. If the objective is a minimization and \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} \subseteq \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}, then

\Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} - J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} \geq 0.

Equality \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = 0 means that the two policy classes attain the same optimal value for the considered objective. It does not imply that the policy classes, the reachable sets, or the viability kernels are identical.

Proof. The inequality is from Proposition E.2. For the equality statement, consider a case where both policy classes attain the same optimal value but are distinct. For example, let the state space be a single state s with two actions a_1 and a_2, both yielding cost 1 and transition to s. Let \Pi_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} allow only a_1, and \Pi_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} allow both a_1 and a_2. Then J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}} = J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}} = 1, but the policy classes are different. \square

E.4 Proof of Theorem 6.9 (Strict Coupling Value)

Theorem E.4. \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}}(s_0) > 0 if and only if there exists an accessible state s' \in V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} \setminus V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}} from which the coupled policy achieves strictly lower cost than any fixed-topology viable policy can achieve from s_0.

Proof. (\Rightarrow) Suppose \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}}(s_0) > 0. Then there exists a coupled viable policy \pi_{\mathrm{cpl}} such that J^{\pi_{\mathrm{cpl}}}(s_0) < J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}}(s_0). Since \pi_{\mathrm{cpl}} is viable, its trajectory remains in V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}}. If the trajectory never enters V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} \setminus V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}}, then the policy is effectively a fixed-topology viable policy, contradicting the strict inequality. Thus there exists an accessible state s' \in V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} \setminus V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}}. From s', the coupled policy achieves a cost strictly lower than any fixed-topology viable policy can achieve from s_0, because otherwise the fixed-topology policy could mimic the coupled policy up to s' and then follow the same actions.

(\Leftarrow) Suppose there exists an accessible state s' \in V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} \setminus V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}} from which the coupled policy achieves strictly lower cost than any fixed-topology viable policy can achieve from s_0. Then the coupled policy from s_0 can reach s' and then exploit the lower cost, yielding J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}(s_0) < J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}}(s_0), so \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}}(s_0) > 0. \square

E.5 Proof of Proposition 6.12 (Structure of Pareto Frontier)

Proposition E.5. For a finite state space, a finite action space, a finite set of policies, and a finite representation of the ambiguity set, the Pareto frontier \{J(\pi) : \pi \in \Pi^*\} is a finite subset of \mathbb{R}^3.

Proof. The set of deterministic stationary policies is finite. The Pareto frontier of a finite set is finite. \square

E.6 Proof of Proposition 6.15 (Value of Information)

Proposition E.6. \mathrm{VoI} = J^{\pi^*}_{\text{partial}} - J^{\pi^*}_{\text{full}} \geq 0. Equality holds if and only if the optimal open-loop policy is optimal closed-loop.

Proof. Under full information, the policy can reproduce any partially informed decision. Under minimization, more information cannot increase the optimal cost. Thus J^{\pi^*}_{\text{full}} \leq J^{\pi^*}_{\text{partial}}, so \mathrm{VoI} \geq 0. Equality holds if and only if the optimal policy under partial information is also optimal under full information, i.e., the optimal open-loop policy is optimal closed-loop. \square

E.7 Counterexample for Remark 6.6

Counterexample E.7. There exists a system where \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = 0 but the viability kernels under fixed and coupled topology are different.

Construction. Let S = \{s_0, s_1, s_2\}. Let G_0 be the initial topology. Let the action set be A = \{a_{\mathrm{fix}}, a_{\mathrm{cpl}}\}. Under a_{\mathrm{fix}}, the transition is s_0 \to s_1 with probability 1, and the cost is 5. Under a_{\mathrm{cpl}}, the transition is s_0 \to s_2 with probability 1, and the cost is 5. Let V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}} = \{s_0, s_1\} and V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}} = \{s_0, s_2\}. Both policies are viable from s_0. Then J_{\mathrm{fix,viab}}(s_0) = 5 and J_{\mathrm{cpl,viab}}(s_0) = 5, so \Delta_{\mathrm{cpl}} = 0. However, the viability kernels are different: V_\infty^{\mathrm{fix}} \neq V_\infty^{\mathrm{cpl}}. \square

