Appendix F — Algorithmic Proofs

F.1 Correctness of Algorithm 7.1 (Backward Recursion)

Theorem F.1. Algorithm 7.1 computes the finite-horizon viability sets V_t correctly.

Proof. By backward induction. Base: V_H = \tilde{K} is correct because at time H the state must be in \tilde{K}. Inductive step: assume V_{t+1} is correct. A state s \in \tilde{K} is in V_t if and only if there exists a \in A(s) such that for all P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a), P(V_{t+1} \mid s,a) = 1. This is exactly the condition checked by the algorithm. \square

F.2 Correctness of Algorithm 7.2 (Infinite-Horizon Kernel)

Theorem F.2. Algorithm 7.2 computes V_\infty correctly.

Proof. By Theorem C.1, the sequence V_n stabilizes at the greatest fixed point V_\infty. The algorithm iterates until V' = V, which occurs exactly at stabilization. \square

F.3 Correctness of Algorithm 7.3 (Optimal Viable Policy)

Theorem F.3. Algorithm 7.3 computes the optimal value V^* and an optimal policy \pi^* for the viability-constrained problem.

Proof. The algorithm performs value iteration on the restricted action sets A_{\mathrm{safe}}(s). By Theorem C.3, the operator is a contraction with modulus \gamma, so value iteration converges to the unique fixed point V^*. The policy \pi^*(s) is chosen as the argmin, which attains the Bellman optimum. \square

F.4 Complexity of Algorithm 7.2

Theorem F.4. Algorithm 7.2 requires at most O(|S|^2 \cdot |A| \cdot |\mathcal{P}|) elementary operations.

Proof. As in Theorem D.4. \square

