Appendix H — Monte Carlo Sampling Bounds

H.1 Hoeffding Inequality

Theorem H.1 (Hoeffding). Let X_1, \dots, X_N be independent random variables with X_i \in [0,1] and \mathbb{E}[X_i] = p. Then for any \delta > 0,

\mathbb{P}\left( \left| \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N X_i - p \right| \geq \delta \right) \leq 2 \exp(-2 N \delta^2).

To achieve error \delta with confidence 1-\alpha, set

2 \exp(-2 N \delta^2) \leq \alpha \implies N \geq \frac{1}{2\delta^2} \log\left( \frac{2}{\alpha} \right).

H.2 Robust Viability Caveat

Sampling under a nominal distribution P_0 \in \mathcal{P}(s,a) does not certify robust viability under all P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a). To certify robust viability, one must sample from the worst-case kernel

P^* = \arg\sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(s,a)} \sum_{s'} P(s' \mid s,a) V(s')

or solve a robust optimization problem over \mathcal{P}(s,a). Nominal simulation can only provide a lower bound on the worst-case probability of remaining in V_\infty.


